# {{ openssl_ca_root_common_name }}

Managed by the Ansible role `ansible-role-openssl-mini-ca`.
Scripts and `*.cnf` files here are overwritten on every playbook run — do not edit in place.

## Layout

    {{ "%-22s"|format(openssl_ca_root_certificate) }}root CA certificate — distribute this
    {{ "%-22s"|format(openssl_ca_root_private_key) }}root CA private key (passphrase-protected)
    {{ "%-22s"|format(openssl_ca_cnf_file) }}CA config, signing policy and extensions
    {{ "%-22s"|format(openssl_server_cnf_file) }}leaf CSR config
    input/                CSRs
    output/               leaf keys and signed certs
    db.certs/             signed certs archived by serial
    db.index              issued certificate database
    db.serial             next serial

## Issue a certificate

Run from `{{ openssl_ca_base_path }}` — the scripts reference the configs by relative path.

    cd {{ openssl_ca_base_path }}
    ./openssl_generate_private_key.sh foo.example.com
    ./openssl_sign.sh foo.example.com

Produces:

    output/foo.example.com.key    unencrypted key, mode 0600
    output/foo.example.com.crt    signed cert, valid {{ openssl_ca_default_days }} days

Default SAN is `DNS:<name>`. Pass an explicit SAN list as the 2nd argument for
multiple names or IP SANs:

    ./openssl_generate_private_key.sh foo.example.com "DNS:foo.example.com,DNS:foo,IP:192.168.1.10"
    ./openssl_sign.sh foo.example.com

To renew, run both scripts again. `unique_subject = no`, so the same CN may be
reissued; the old `output/` key and cert are overwritten without warning and a new
`db.index` entry is added while the previous one stays valid until it expires.

## Install the CA on clients

Debian/Ubuntu:

    cp {{ openssl_ca_root_certificate }} /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/{{ openssl_ca_root_common_name | lower }}.crt
    update-ca-certificates

RHEL:

    cp {{ openssl_ca_root_certificate }} /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/{{ openssl_ca_root_common_name | lower }}.crt
    update-ca-trust

## Inspect

    openssl x509 -in output/foo.example.com.crt -noout -text
    openssl x509 -in {{ openssl_ca_root_certificate }} -noout -subject -dates
    openssl verify -CAfile {{ openssl_ca_root_certificate }} output/foo.example.com.crt
    cat db.index

`db.index` columns: status (`V`/`R`/`E`), expiry, revocation date, serial, filename, subject.

## Revoke

The CA passphrase is embedded in `openssl_sign.sh`.

    cd {{ openssl_ca_base_path }}
    openssl ca -config {{ openssl_ca_cnf_file }} -revoke db.certs/<SERIAL>.pem -passin pass:<PASSPHRASE>
    openssl ca -config {{ openssl_ca_cnf_file }} -gencrl -out crl.pem -passin pass:<PASSPHRASE>

CRL is valid {{ openssl_ca_default_crl_days }} days; regenerate before it expires if anything consumes it.

## Notes

- Root CA is valid {{ openssl_ca_root_days }} days from creation.
- Leaf certs carry no `extendedKeyUsage`; Apple clients may reject them for TLS.
- `copy_extensions = copy` is on — SANs come from the CSR. `signing_req` forces
  `CA:FALSE` and the key usage, so a CSR cannot request them.
